-- title:
--   Descent to a countable algebraically closed constant field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$ and $F$ a field with a $K$-algebra structure, subject to two hypotheses: there is an $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$, and $F$ is a curve over $K$ in the project's sense, i.e. every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a divisor $D$ of degree $0$ with $D v = v.\mathrm{ord}\, f$ at every place $v$ of $F/K$, every such place has residue field finite-dimensional over $K$, and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. Let $t$ be a finite subset of $F$. Then there are a field $K_0$ in the universe of $K$ and a field $F_0$ in that of $F$, carrying algebra structures $K_0 \to F_0$, $K_0 \to K$, $F_0 \to F$, $K_0 \to F$ with the two scalar towers $K_0/K/F$ and $K_0/F_0/F$ compatible, together with an algebra structure $K_0 \to \mathbb{C}$, such that $K_0$ is algebraically closed and countable, $F_0$ is a curve over $K_0$ in the same sense, some element of $F_0$ is transcendental over $K_0$ with $F_0$ finite-dimensional over the subfield it generates, the image of $F_0$ in $F$ generates $F$ over $K$ (the intermediate field adjoining the range of $F_0 \to F$ to $K$ is everything), and every element of $t$ lies in the image of $F_0$.
--
--   This is the constant-field descent step for a function field in one variable: the given curve $F/K$ is realised as the constant-field extension of a curve $F_0/K_0$ over a countable algebraically closed subfield admitting an embedding into $\mathbb{C}$, with finitely many prescribed elements of $F$ arranged to come from $F_0$ (so that, for instance, uniformisers at prescribed places descend). It is used in the proof of [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_natCast_dvd_ord_sub_single_sub_single`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_natCast_dvd_ord_sub_single_sub_single), where a statement about orders of functions at places is transferred to the complex-analytic situation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_exists_constantFieldDescent_finset.lean

